# Collaborator Log — {{GITHUB_USERNAME}}

A personal, dated work log for {{GITHUB_USERNAME}} on this project — one
file per collaborator, kept separate on purpose. A single shared log
everyone appends to conflicts on nearly every concurrent PR (two people
editing the same lines of the same file); per-person files avoid that
structurally, the same reason this kit already treats `setup-hooks.sh`
and `verify-setup.sh` as separate files rather than one shared script.

**What this is for:** letting anyone — a PR reviewer, a collaborator
picking up related work, a future session with no memory of this one —
read one person's own account of what they did and why, signed, without
reconstructing it from git history alone.

**What this is NOT:** a replacement for real PR descriptions, commit
messages, or this kit's other shared living documents
(`OPEN-RESEARCH-QUEUE.md`, a signed-off `STAGE-0-RESEARCH-ENRICHMENT.md`).
This is the *personal* record; those are the *project's* shared record.
Don't duplicate a PR's own description here — reference it, and record
what a PR description doesn't capture (why, in your own words, not just
what).

**Real limitation, stated plainly, not glossed over:** if an AI agent
works under this same GitHub identity (shared credentials — see this
project's own `PASS-0-ENVIRONMENT-DISCOVERY.md` for whether that's the
case here), git and GitHub cannot mechanically tell an entry the human
wrote apart from one an agent wrote on their behalf. This file's own
content is the only place that distinction can survive — **say plainly
in each entry which one it was** ("written by [name]" vs. "this
session's work was Claude Sonnet 5, directed by [name]"), rather than
letting every entry read as equally human-authored when some weren't.

**Don't infer who a log entry belongs to from session context.** A
session's `userEmail`-style field, a git `user.email`, or an OS username
reflects the local account/session, not necessarily a different real
person (a real session took one human's changed local account for a
second contributor). Which person is working is established by an
explicit statement from them or by a PR/review from their own
account, not by guessing from environment fields.

**Mechanical nudge, not a gate:** a CI check (if this project copied
`templates/workflows/collaborator-log-reminder.yml.template`) posts a
non-blocking reminder if a PR doesn't touch this file — informational
only, same "detection not prevention" shape as this kit's other checks
on a repo without branch protection. It does not, and cannot, verify
that an entry is honest or complete.

